To log all outgoing HTTP requests in a Spring Boot application, the most straightforward approach is to use Spring's `WebClient` with Reactor's `Mono.log()`. Here's a step-by-step guide:

### 1. Add Dependencies

Ensure you have the WebFlux and WebClient starters:

```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webflux</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

### 2. Configure WebClient to Log Requests and Responses

Create a configuration class or modify your existing `WebClient` setup:

```java
import org.reactivestreams.Publisher;
import org.springframework.boot.web.reactive.function.ClientHttpRequestFactoryCustomizer;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.client.reactive.ReactorClientHttpConnector;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientResponse;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ExchangeFilterFunction;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;

import java.util.function.Function;

@Configuration
public class WebClientConfig {

    @Bean
    public WebClient webClient(ClientHttpRequestFactoryCustomizer customizer) {
        customizer(cf -> cf.setConnectionFactory(
            new ReactorClientHttpConnector(
                java.net.HttpClientBuilders.create().build()
            )
        ));

        return WebClient.builder()
            .filter(ExchangeFilterFunction.ofRequestModification(
                request -> request.headers().add("Accept", "application/json")
            ))
            .filter(ExchangeFilterFunction.ofResponseTransformation(
                response -> {
                    System.out.println("Response Status: " + response.status());
                    return response.bodyToFlux(String.class).doOnNext(System.out::println);
                }
            ))
            .build();
    }
}
```

For even simpler logging, you can wrap your calls like this:

```java
public class LoggingWebClient {

    private final WebClient webClient;

    public LoggingWebClient(WebClient.Builder builder) {
        this.webClient = builder
            .codecs(configurer -> configurer.defaultCodecs().encoder(CustomEncoder.class, MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON, EncoderCodecCompat.WRAPPERS))
            .build();
    }

    public <T> Mono<T> get(String uri) {
        return webClient.get()
            .uri(uri)
            .retrieve()
            .onStatus(HttpStatus::is4xxClientError, response -> Mono.error(new HttpClientException("Client error: " + response.status())))
            .onStatus(HttpStatus::is5xxServerError, response -> Mono.error(new HttpClientException("Server error: " + response.status())))
            .doOnSuccess(response -> System.out.println("Request succeeded: " + response.status()))
            .doOnError(throwable -> System.out.println("Request failed: " + throwable.getMessage()))
            .map(this::logResponse);
    }

    private <T> T logResponse(T body) {
        System.out.println("Response body: " + body);
        return body;
    }

    private class CustomEncoder implements Encoder<String, Object> {
        @Override
        public Encoder<String, Object> forType(Class<? extends Object> type) {
            return new Encoder<String, Object>() {
                @Override
                public String encode(String value, Object object, MediaType mediaType) {
                    return object.toString();
                }
            };
        }
    }
}
```

